//? Title: Linear Regression: Tensor SGD and Normal Equations
//? Subtitle: Solve for coefficients without forming an explicit inverse
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A linear predictor must separate explainable variation from residual error, while avoiding
numerically unstable coefficient estimates. We fit a five-point problem with explicit tensor SGD,
use QR as an independent optimum check, and optimise a small real-data model. Residual
orthogonality gives a check independent of coefficient values.
)
\toc

> The method

Minimising L(w)=||Xw-y||²/(2N) gives gradient Xᵀ(Xw-y)/N. A full-column-rank optimum
therefore has residual orthogonal to every design column. Normal equations solve XᵀXw=Xᵀy
by Cholesky; thin QR instead writes X=QR and solves Rw=Qᵀy. Neither needs an explicit inverse.

$$
\nabla L=X^\top(Xw-y)/N,\qquad \kappa_2(X^\top X)=\kappa_2(X)^2.
$$
\alttext(The loss gradient is the design-transposed residual divided by sample size; forming a Gram matrix squares the spectral condition number.)

QR is preferable on nearly dependent columns because it avoids forming that Gram matrix.
Gradient descent converges on a fixed full-rank quadratic when 0<rate<2/lambda_max(XᵀX/N).
Direct dense solving costs O(Np²+p³), while a gradient step costs O(Np). The rank and
conditioning assumptions matter; a unique prediction fit need not imply stable individual
coefficients near collinearity.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/linear_regression_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/linear_regression_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/linear_regression_concept.tikz > build/linear_regression_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/linear_regression_concept.svg build/linear_regression_concept.dvi > build/linear_regression_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/linear_regression_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
